          },
          "mechanism": "Parity directly affects hormonal exposure over the life course and alters long-term metabolic and immune function. Pregnancies themselves cause physiologic changes that can affect both menstrual cycle patterns and respiratory function.",
          "justification": "Parity is associated with both exposure (pregnant women experience cycle changes) and outcome (pregnancy history affects later health), and adjustment improves precision in this female-only cohort.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q2.10",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:b188718a8d8144f5fe4cf3c35a60b74088dcd97a8cf89ca2f1fec01a9af8139b"
          },
          "mechanism": "Body weight affects estrogen metabolism (obesity increases circulating estrogen through adipose tissue production) and is strongly associated with menstrual cycle characteristics. Weight also directly affects respiratory mechanics, inflammation, and COPD risk.",
          "justification": "Body weight is a common cause of variation in both cycle length and respiratory disease.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m1:Q3.10",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:780728bf35035558d044eb83b0a0913fa9ac23432800ba7771332210ddb7cedd"
          },
          "mechanism": "Race captures differences in genetic predisposition to hormonal and respiratory phenotypes, environmental exposures (including occupational and air quality), healthcare access and diagnostic practices (affecting whether COPD diagnosis is made), and socioeconomic factors that influence both health behaviors and disease development.",
          "justification": "Race is associated with variation in exposure and outcome through multiple pathways including genetic predisposition, environmental exposures, and healthcare access.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m3:Q5.5",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:de9dcac5ebc6d726296a67ec78524bb13c5da9982763c0234ce9ca8653cc6c0a"
          },
          "mechanism": "Cigarette smoking is the leading cause of COPD and also affects reproductive hormone metabolism. Smoking exposure directly damages lungs and increases COPD risk while simultaneously affecting menstrual function through endocrine disruption.",
          "justification": "Cigarette smoking is a strong common cause of both altered menstrual patterns and emphysema/COPD development.",
          "role": "confounder",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        }
      ],
